      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to determine the probability that the two-digit number \\(ab\\) (where \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are digits obtained from tossing a fair 6-sided die) and both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3. Since a fair 6-sided die has 6 possible outcomes (\\(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\)), each outcome is equally likely to occur.\n\nWe will check the digits for each outcome and see if both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3:\n\n- For the outcome \\(1\\), the digits are \\(1\\) and \\(1\\).\n- For the outcome \\(2\\), the digits are \\(2\\) and \\(2\\).\n- For the outcome \\(3\\), the digits are \\(3\\) and \\(3\\).\n- For the outcome \\(4\\), the digits are \\(4\\) and \\(4\\).\n- For the outcome \\(5\\), the digits are \\(5\\) and \\(5\\).\n- For the outcome \\(6\\), the digits are \\(6\\) and \\(6\\).\n\nWe need to check which of these digits are divisible by 3:\n\n- \\(1\\) and \\(2\\) are not divisible by 3.\n- \\(3\\) is divisible by 3.\n- \\(4\\) and \\(5\\) are not divisible by 3.\n- \\(6\\) and \\(6\\) are divisible by 3.\n\nSo, the outcomes that meet the criteria are \\(3\\) and \\(6\\).\n\nSince each die outcome is equally likely, the probability that the sum \\(ab\\) is divisible by 3 and both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3 is the ratio of the successful outcomes to the total outcomes. Here, the successful outcomes are \\(3\\) and \\(6\\), and there are 12 possible outcomes in total (each of the 6 possible outcomes on the first die and each of the 6 possible outcomes on the second die).\n\nThus, the probability is given by\n\n[ \\frac{2}{12} = \\frac{1}{6} ]\n\nSo the final answer is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{6}}\\).",
